WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.100
A quantum entanglement experiment

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.580
performed on this satellite

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.390
showed that shared quantum states

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.670
can extend over 1,200 kilometers,

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.940
smashing the previous world record.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.040
Quantum entanglement is physics at its strangest.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.120
Subatomic particles like photons can be entangled

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.220
by sharing their quantum states,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.600
such as their spin and polarization.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.990
Even if the photons are separated by vast distances,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.890
these shared states should remain in limbo

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.440
until one of the objects is measured or disturbed.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.730
Then, the quantum states for both photons

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.039
suddenly collapse to a set value--

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.440
with no information passing between the two.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.600
With fiber optics, scientists have been able

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.469
to demonstrate quantum entanglement on Earth

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:49.860
across a few hundred kilometers.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.210
Any farther out, and the photons’ quantum states

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.059
are upset by fluctuations in the atmosphere.

00:00:56.059 --> 00:00:58.149
But photons travel much more smoothly

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:00.730
in the near-vacuum of space.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:03.679
That’s where the Micius--the first space-based

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:06.860
quantum research platform--comes in.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.870
Inside Micius, an ultraviolet laser is split

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.220
and sent into a special crystal

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.910
that produces entangled photons

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.190
with opposite polarization states.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.250
The pairs are then beamed down to earth--

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.960
5.9 million entangled pairs per second.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.970
Two ground stations in China, over 1200 km apart,

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.520
receive the photons.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.020
When The scientists, measured the states

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.400
of the incoming photons, they were correlated

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.440
far more often than would be expected

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.040
by random chance --

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.070
evidence that the photons had remained entangled

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:40.880
across large distances.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.040
Although the transmission of the entangled photons

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.000
was much more efficient

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.980
than ground-based experiments, still, just

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.090
Next the researchers plan to distribute quantum keys

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.530
to the stations--

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:02.300
which requires long strings of entangled photons.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.390
With entangled encryption keys,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.220
two people in separate locations

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.040
could communicate secretly with each other

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.420
and any attempt at eavesdropping would be noticed.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.120
Researchers plan to extend the reach

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.100
of the fledgling quantum network

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.219
by adding more satellites and ground stations.

00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:20.459
That would be a stepping stone to a space-based

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:25.400
world-wide quantum internet.

